#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265641 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f9683d9-05b9-5a42-9f81-0170a15dfac1" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Double-Angle Formula to Simplify Ratios"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f9fa087f-f6db-5d41-bce6-176e0dfc84bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265643} {:answer/id #uuid "8291aef2-a55a-5035-960c-b2fd32db51b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265644} {:answer/id #uuid "74e0f6fd-d7a9-5e32-b935-3d2d63d15eee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265645} {:answer/id #uuid "16abd149-1622-5870-acac-08550572af4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265646} {:answer/id #uuid "0d7a1573-25a2-57b7-9e53-7a5153bbcccf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265647}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d7a1573-25a2-57b7-9e53-7a5153bbcccf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265647} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e71bada-abf2-5012-a4dd-26de4973c091" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265642}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0d2067af-e1b2-5f04-ba26-5b2307cb8aa1" :question/math-academy-id "q-120017" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}).\n$$\n\nRewriting the given expression using the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})} \\\\  & =\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
